@danwilliamsphil made a similar point, and it is fair. To me the button-pressing results are unsurprising.  The reason I find the button-pressing etc unsurprising ex-post is that the training data is not merely descriptive of the human emotion of pain, but it also contains a lot of information about what people will do in order to avoid pain. The instrumental responses are embedded in the training data too.

The model-directedness is more interesting but I think can also be explained ex-post in terms of the utility of the LLM forming some representations of 'self' vs 'other' again on the basis of the training data.

In short, I think we're always on tricky ground when deciding whether LLM behaviour is surprising or not, given the richness of the training data & especially the richness of the self-directed narratives within these data.

